WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.839
Today I'm speaking to 21 year old
Neelakantha Bhanu, who won the gold medal for

00:00:05.839 --> 00:00:14.222
India at the 2020 Mind Sports Olympiad, and he
holds a few world records for being the world's

00:00:14.222 --> 00:00:16.099
fastest human calculator.

00:00:30.947 --> 00:00:36.786
Toby: I think we first got in contact with each other
after I made a video about Shakuntala Devi, from

00:00:36.786 --> 00:00:43.835
India, and she previously I think, held this
record for you know, Fastest Human Calculator. 

00:00:43.835 --> 00:00:50.341
And yeah, I'm wondering, have you beaten her
record? And if so, what was that record?

00:00:50.341 --> 00:00:57.891
Bhanu: Shakuntala Devi was, let's say, one of the
first mathemagicians if I have to put it this way,

00:00:58.099 --> 00:01:04.063
who has established math as a performance act,
which is that going out on stages, making people

00:01:04.063 --> 00:01:09.027
clap, making people enjoy the art of, let's say, 
solving questions in front of them. 

00:01:09.027 --> 00:01:12.822
She held a lot of world records. 
But there's this one specific world record, 

00:01:12.822 --> 00:01:20.497
which is called the Fastest Human Calculator record, 
where one gets to add a number to itself as quickly as possible. 

00:01:20.497 --> 00:01:27.295
If it's a two digit number, if I have to put it this way, 
one has to do the multiples of it very quickly, and say the answers out. 

00:01:27.295 --> 00:01:33.134
So if I start with 28, 
it goes 28, 56, 84, 112, 140, 168, and so on, so on. 

00:01:33.134 --> 00:01:37.514
So the record is to do as many times in 15 seconds. 

00:01:37.514 --> 00:01:43.144
So these records were once held by her, Mr. Scott
Flansburgh from the United States, and that's the

00:01:43.144 --> 00:01:51.069
record which I've broken a couple of years ago, in
racing past and doing it for 42 times in 15 seconds.

00:01:51.069 --> 00:01:55.365
I've seen some videos of it done, and yeah, I find it really impressive. 

00:01:55.365 --> 00:02:02.288
And in fact, when I first read the story of Shakuntala, 
and then heard your story as well, 

00:02:02.288 --> 00:02:09.629
My initial thoughts, and maybe these are the thoughts
of many people, it's like; wow, that is such a

00:02:09.671 --> 00:02:14.008
special talent. And those people seem so different
to me.

00:02:14.008 --> 00:02:20.640
Because, you know, I feel like I could never do
that and I think many people maybe feel the same.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:26.104
I wonder if you could answer you know, is there
something special about you guys who can do this?

00:02:26.104 --> 00:02:32.402
Bhanu: I personally do not think that this is a
feat or a feature which can be achieved by being

00:02:32.402 --> 00:02:39.033
gifted, or being let's say naturally innately
talented. Because of one sure reason that

00:02:39.242 --> 00:02:44.956
everyone's born with the same, let's say
mathematical, what do we say? raw materials? 

00:02:45.206 --> 00:02:52.922
It’s just about how you train it. And my question
again, which again, I think I'll do more as we go,

00:02:52.922 --> 00:03:01.389
which is to compare it, compare math with being
sport is that if one wants a ripped body with a

00:03:01.389 --> 00:03:06.978
tight six pack, is it really possible for
everyone? Yes. But it does take the effort, which

00:03:06.978 --> 00:03:09.939
is to train, to diet, do a bunch of stuff.

00:03:09.981 --> 00:03:14.777
Toby: Yeah. So it sounds like you're saying it
takes, you know, the willingness and the

00:03:14.777 --> 00:03:22.452
motivation to want to do this, and also a lot of
hard work. Yeah, I guess training like any classic

00:03:22.493 --> 00:03:29.000
olympian for for any other sport, I guess. So you
know, I'd be curious to know, when you were

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:36.758
training for this, what was your sort of daily
routine? How did you find the time to do this kind

00:03:36.758 --> 00:03:37.467
of study?

00:03:38.176 --> 00:03:44.140
Bhanu: So I mean, my journey in mental calculation
started when I was very young, very, very young.

00:03:44.182 --> 00:03:49.604
So I actually met with an accident when I was
five, when I was bedridden for an entire year.

00:03:49.854 --> 00:03:55.276
That has nothing to do with my calculation
abilities. But at that point of time, I picked up

00:03:55.276 --> 00:04:00.406
puzzles and calculations as a way which is, be it
magic squares, be it a bunch of other stuff, which

00:04:00.406 --> 00:04:06.829
we genuinely, let's say, see in our daily routine,
right? So I mean, this was the point of time where

00:04:06.871 --> 00:04:12.794
I sort of picked and started falling in love with
what puzzles and math and numbers and stuff are. I

00:04:12.835 --> 00:04:18.132
fumbled on to, let's say, taking mental
calculation as something which I was interested in

00:04:18.174 --> 00:04:24.013
and started going for regional championships, and
I won the regionals, nationals and the

00:04:24.013 --> 00:04:29.769
international championships in the span of three
years. Directly, I was the international champion

00:04:29.811 --> 00:04:36.734
in speed calculation under the age of 20 years, by
the time I was 13. That was in Singapore. My

00:04:36.734 --> 00:04:43.366
routine when I did practice for this was I used to
go to school, come back around 4pm in the evening,

00:04:43.366 --> 00:04:49.622
and then sort of work because I mean, at the end
of the day, I would consider mental math to be a

00:04:49.622 --> 00:04:55.670
sport, which again, gives it a value of
entertainment, right? So I mean, for me, it never

00:04:55.670 --> 00:05:01.634
was that; okay, sit in front of the paper, solve
this exam. It was about give this attempt, try

00:05:01.634 --> 00:05:09.517
doing this better, play better. So it's the kind
of satisfaction or the sense of achievement you

00:05:09.517 --> 00:05:16.774
get when you get your soccer balls swing in the
air and that's pretty much what what drove me

00:05:16.774 --> 00:05:21.487
forward. I used to practice around six to seven
hours a day, consistently for three to four years.

00:05:21.529 --> 00:05:27.618
Today, I don't practice as much, but I still make
it a point that I'm active for at least one, one

00:05:27.618 --> 00:05:32.540
and a half hour every day to sort of look into
this and pursue this further. Because I think this

00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:39.130
has answers to a lot of things which human
cognition has to scope in and do more in.

00:05:40.048 --> 00:05:46.471
Toby: Great, yeah, one thing I'd be curious to ask
is that, you know, on my channel, I've done a few

00:05:46.471 --> 00:05:53.102
videos in the past relating to exams and
specifically exams in India, things like the JEE

00:05:53.102 --> 00:05:58.900
to get into university. And before we were
recording, you said that you've gone on to study

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:05.114
maths at university. So clearly, you know, at the
same time you were studying for this, you know,

00:06:05.114 --> 00:06:11.496
mental maths, you were also studying your normal
schoolwork, right? And did you ever sit the JEE

00:06:11.496 --> 00:06:16.042
and have to do exams at the same time? How was
that, balancing them?

00:06:17.126 --> 00:06:25.510
Bhanu: I really did well in my JEE. In fact, I
actually scored a 115/120 in my JEE in math. So

00:06:27.011 --> 00:06:34.727
again right, I would look at it in two directions,
which is mathematical acumen building can happen

00:06:34.727 --> 00:06:39.982
from any side. It's about, I personally look at
this as a mountain, you can climb it from any

00:06:39.982 --> 00:06:46.781
forte, but the end is mathematical excellence. And
there's no way you miss out on things as you

00:06:46.781 --> 00:06:52.161
climb. So I mean, for instance, if I am doing a
speed calculation and at some point of time I

00:06:52.161 --> 00:06:56.749
wanted to do an approximation, there is no way I
can do that without knowing the Taylor series,

00:06:56.958 --> 00:07:04.757
there is no way I can do that without knowing how
do I extrapolate and sort of do a bunch of other

00:07:04.757 --> 00:07:09.679
things. When I'm, let's say, approximating a few
calculations there's a lot of ideologies which go

00:07:09.679 --> 00:07:15.977
behind it, be it, let's say conic sections, be it
a lot of other stuff, which I think is a part of

00:07:15.977 --> 00:07:20.982
what we do, number theory majorly. So, prime
factorization, if that's the calculation I'm going

00:07:20.982 --> 00:07:29.073
for. I learned a lot of formal math, through the
problems I faced while I scaled on becoming the

00:07:29.073 --> 00:07:33.619
world's fastest human calculator. So I will say
that my journey in math was intertwined.

00:07:33.703 --> 00:07:39.667
Toby: I really liked the comment that you made a
bit before about kind of what you had learned

00:07:39.917 --> 00:07:46.174
about the human brain and about learning itself
through your experiences. And I wonder if you

00:07:46.174 --> 00:07:52.054
could talk maybe a little bit more about that,
because maybe that kind of gives a usefulness as

00:07:52.054 --> 00:07:58.352
well to what you're doing. Not that it, you know,
needs a usefulness because I know many sports you

00:07:58.352 --> 00:08:04.609
do just for the sake of showing what's possible,
but it seems like here, the mental calculation

00:08:04.609 --> 00:08:09.071
stuff can kind of reveal a bit more about how we
work, how we think.

00:08:09.071 --> 00:08:16.787
Bhanu: Noam Chomsky, a very famous linguist says
that you sort of look at the world, the way you

00:08:16.787 --> 00:08:20.833
learn language, which is watching the world
through the language glass, which is one of the

00:08:20.833 --> 00:08:24.795
most revolutionary works between language and cognition.

00:08:24.795 --> 00:08:33.638
I personally think that language and the way you
learn numbers sort of determine how you look at

00:08:33.638 --> 00:08:40.144
the world in general. And if I have to put it this
way, from research which has been done by Piaget,

00:08:40.228 --> 00:08:46.234
or a lot of other mathematician-philosophers have
worked on this, or mathematical psychologists,

00:08:46.943 --> 00:08:54.533
it's observed more so now than ever, that the way
some kid or a student learns the number system,

00:08:54.992 --> 00:09:01.749
the way they learn the operations of math, the way
they learn how naturally one flows from the other

00:09:01.791 --> 00:09:08.923
logical argumentation and stuff sort of determines
their cognitive compass. If I have to say this

00:09:08.923 --> 00:09:13.552
that way. When I say cognitive compass, I'm
talking about the working memory, the iterative

00:09:13.552 --> 00:09:18.224
memory, the long term memory, the reflex, the
ability to partition thought, the ability to

00:09:18.224 --> 00:09:24.522
parallel think, a lot of things here, and if I
have to compare it with let's say, computers, or

00:09:24.522 --> 00:09:29.652
let's say how a computer functions, it's about how
you develop your RAM. How you develop your your

00:09:29.652 --> 00:09:34.490
GPU, how you develop your processor, however that
goes.

00:09:34.490 --> 00:09:41.831
Toby: Yeah, I guess on that, and also bringing
back the, you know, parallels to sports, why do

00:09:41.831 --> 00:09:48.421
you think that you are able to beat for example,
Shakuntala's record? And I asked that because, you

00:09:48.421 --> 00:09:54.427
know, in sports, in some sports, maybe marathon
running or something else, you see that over time,

00:09:54.635 --> 00:10:00.433
people seem to be getting faster. Maybe it's the
shoes or the swimsuits they're wearing or maybe

00:10:00.433 --> 00:10:05.521
it's the way we train. But do you think in a
similar way that people are getting smarter? Is

00:10:05.521 --> 00:10:07.732
that why records are being broken here?

00:10:09.650 --> 00:10:16.699
Bhanu: Sure, I could probably do that. Because I
mean, I personally think my practice majorly has

00:10:16.699 --> 00:10:24.582
not been through pen and paper solutions. It has
been aided by software a lot. And I would say that

00:10:24.582 --> 00:10:30.421
that's something which, I think plays a very key
role. It's this way, right? I mean, there's two

00:10:30.421 --> 00:10:35.468
ways to approach let's say, running for that same
marathon, which is, one is to keep running for

00:10:35.468 --> 00:10:42.224
that 100 metres expecting growth, or do 10k runs,
do runs with weight, do runs with something else,

00:10:42.224 --> 00:10:45.770
train yourself in 10 different things. So that
eventually when you come back to this, you are

00:10:45.770 --> 00:10:51.150
better. Mental calculation, and the art of it is
slowly in decline, which is, I mean, when I look

00:10:51.150 --> 00:10:56.864
forward, when I when I talk to let's say 60 year
old war veterans, 70 year old World War veterans

00:10:56.864 --> 00:11:01.702
across the globe, or, I mean, whenever I go and
talk, they're way quicker at what they do than

00:11:01.702 --> 00:11:10.544
let's say, people of my age, or under because of
the reliance on calculators a lot, which is great.

00:11:10.544 --> 00:11:15.299
For instance, I mean, no one really wants to
calculate cost 46 and a half. We don't really want

00:11:15.299 --> 00:11:20.388
to do that, because there's machines doing it for
us. So when I compare it to the sport, this is

00:11:20.388 --> 00:11:25.267
exactly what I say. And I think I'll probably
repeat it here, which establishes the point. Usain

00:11:25.267 --> 00:11:32.233
Bolt runs for 100 metres, right. The reason he
runs for the 100 metres is to make sure to inspire

00:11:32.233 --> 00:11:38.447
people to say that, okay, see, this is what being
fit is. It's not that he does not have a car. So

00:11:38.447 --> 00:11:42.827
it's not that he does not have a car. So I would
probably end up with that saying that, if you

00:11:42.910 --> 00:11:46.872
today, if you're here, you should probably be
using Wolfram Alpha. If you're here, you should be

00:11:46.872 --> 00:11:52.378
using your MATLAB and Mathematica. If you're not,
you're not in the math game. But at the same time,

00:11:52.378 --> 00:11:56.841
it's extremely important for you to train your
brain. And there's no better exercise than

00:11:56.841 --> 00:12:04.306
counting. And trust me. That's literally what I
put here. Brain exercise is, I personally think

00:12:04.306 --> 00:12:09.520
that math, if you lose out on calculations to be a
way in which you train yourself, you're sort of

00:12:09.979 --> 00:12:12.440
missing out one part of the jigsaw puzzle.

00:12:12.648 --> 00:12:18.863
Toby: It's really interesting to hear that perhaps
technology has played a role in helping you to

00:12:18.863 --> 00:12:19.947
break records.

00:12:20.072 --> 00:12:24.368
I guess I'd love to hear a little bit more about
you know, what you're doing now, your current

00:12:24.368 --> 00:12:24.910
mission?

00:12:25.161 --> 00:12:32.710
Bhanu: Math failure is one of the major reasons
why students drop out of school, which is to drop

00:12:32.710 --> 00:12:39.842
out of any other educational trajectory they can
have. So I think the problem which I do go on, and

00:12:39.842 --> 00:12:46.015
I probably wish to solve is sort of at least make
math not the speed breaker for someone's career

00:12:46.015 --> 00:12:53.606
opportunity, because it doesn't, it shouldn't be
that hard. And if you let's say inspire people,

00:12:54.148 --> 00:12:59.153
because I think that the problem is twofold; one
is that it's the social perception that math is

00:12:59.153 --> 00:13:04.617
boring, which I think it's existent regardless of
the country I'm talking about 'oh not math, not

00:13:04.617 --> 00:13:05.409
really, not my thing'.

00:13:06.327 --> 00:13:07.620
'Oh, you do math, you're a geek'.

00:13:07.620 --> 00:13:12.792
So I'm trying to change that by let's say, doing
performances and stage shows across the globe.

00:13:12.875 --> 00:13:18.798
Toby: This, the competitive side of, you know,
human calculation, it will really help some people

00:13:18.798 --> 00:13:24.011
to get inspired with maths. For some people, that
won't be quite right, because it might bring that,

00:13:24.094 --> 00:13:28.474
you know, the sense of pressure and
competitiveness that comes along with exams, too.

00:13:28.474 --> 00:13:33.437
It can it can actually sometimes be detrimental.
So I think one approach will not work for

00:13:33.437 --> 00:13:39.735
everyone. This has clearly worked for you and,
yeah, I would like to add a little disclaimer

00:13:39.735 --> 00:13:47.201
that, you know, I personally studied math at uni
as well. And I found that yeah, you can get by

00:13:47.201 --> 00:13:52.915
just fine without being good at arithmetic, and
it's not really going to hold you back. But it's

00:13:52.915 --> 00:13:57.962
interesting to hear your story of a case where
it's actually really helped propel you forward.

00:13:58.045 --> 00:14:03.050
Bhanu: Math phobia exists, I mean, let's accept
it, that there is a scare of every math exam,

00:14:03.050 --> 00:14:10.015
which comes your way. The two ways to go about it.
One is to, as you said, I mean, I think as you

00:14:10.015 --> 00:14:17.398
pointed out also, that competitively getting it
overwith is not always the best way to go about

00:14:17.398 --> 00:14:23.654
it. Also, because of the fact that if you really
want to write a book, you don't really have to be

00:14:23.654 --> 00:14:31.745
a spell bee champion. It's as simple as that. But
both of them have their own beautiful forms and I

00:14:31.745 --> 00:14:39.336
believe that moving forward  we have more people
who sort of work towards catering such experiences

00:14:39.336 --> 00:14:46.385
globally, where kids no longer are scared of math,
but are accepted. They accept it as, let's say, a

00:14:46.385 --> 00:14:51.015
part of their life just as we do with English or
any other native language you speak.

00:14:51.682 --> 00:14:58.022
Toby: Great. Yeah, it's a big mission, but I think
it's a good one. Yeah, one little question I'd

00:14:58.022 --> 00:15:04.653
like to end with is do you have maybe a role model
or someone that you really look up to maybe from

00:15:04.653 --> 00:15:05.779
history or now?

00:15:07.531 --> 00:15:14.622
Bhanu: Actually a lot of mathematicians, but let
me restrict to a couple of them. One very, very

00:15:14.622 --> 00:15:22.254
major inspiration through my life from the
beginning, how much ever I understood him or not,

00:15:22.296 --> 00:15:29.970
from the beginning was Srinivasa Ramanujan, from
India. And, I mean, I would say that my devotion

00:15:29.970 --> 00:15:39.813
towards his work is, is I think, goes a long way
because of the fact that, okay, one is obviously

00:15:39.813 --> 00:15:48.238
the cultural, the sort of Indian-ness and the
promotion of what he has done globally. But the

00:15:48.238 --> 00:15:53.243
sheer nature of let's say, every time I look up
into, let's say, read more, I realise that oh,

00:15:53.243 --> 00:15:58.290
this is more apt to what I do. So I mean, wherever
I was fumbling across a bunch of things, I've

00:15:58.290 --> 00:16:05.673
somehow always come to a certain paper of his
which I'm like, oh, wow, this, I think is

00:16:05.714 --> 00:16:10.344
applicable to what I do. So I will say that he was
one of my major inspirations.

00:16:10.803 --> 00:16:18.644
Around, let's say, performances and making math a
culture or let's say something which people

00:16:18.644 --> 00:16:24.525
consume in India, I'm thankful for Shakuntala
Devi. She was never a formal mathematics person

00:16:24.566 --> 00:16:34.201
herself. She was never formally trained. But I
think the relatability of a mathematician to an

00:16:34.201 --> 00:16:43.335
average citizen of a country is I think, something
which, which I think I've learned a lot from.

00:16:43.502 --> 00:16:49.508
Toby: Great. Well, yeah, I find both of those
people also very inspirational. So, yeah,

00:16:50.217 --> 00:16:55.431
Bhanu: I mean, nameless to say the other couple of
people include Laplace and Newton. I mean, I've

00:16:55.431 --> 00:16:58.892
not gone there. But yeah, but I've not gone there
now. Yeah.

00:16:58.892 --> 00:17:04.565
Toby: Some of the greats. Yeah. All right. Well,
congratulations on all of your success. And thanks

00:17:04.565 --> 00:17:06.191
for sharing your insights.

00:17:06.900 --> 00:17:09.737
Bhanu: Thank you. Thanks a lot. It was nice, nice
being here.

